<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chutia people</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Chutia are an ethnic group of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assam">Assam</a> in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, of Bodo-Kachari origin. Their medieval kingdom, centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sadiya">Sadiya</a> in the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmaputra">Brahmaputra</a> valley, flourished from about the 13th century until the Ahom king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suhungmung">Suhungmung</a> annexed it in 1523–24. Many Chutias were then absorbed into Ahom society, and today they form a recognised community speaking mainly Assamese.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chutia-people.jpg" alt="Chutia people" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Barbariankiller456 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Women_of_Chutia_tribe_preparing_pithas.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
